C++ syntax illustrating RVO/NRVO:

```cpp
class ExpensiveClass {
public:
  ExpensiveClass() { /* expensive construction */ }
  ExpensiveClass(const ExpensiveClass& other) { /* expensive copy construction */ }
  ~ExpensiveClass() { /* expensive destruction */ }
};

ExpensiveClass createExpensiveObject() {
  ExpensiveClass obj;
  // ... some operations on obj ...
  return obj; // RVO/NRVO can happen here
}

int main() {
  ExpensiveClass myObj = createExpensiveObject();
  return 0;
}
```

**Compiler-specific notes:** The effectiveness of RVO/NRVO varies.  Compilers like g++ and clang++ often aggressively perform these optimizations, especially at higher optimization levels (-O2, -O3).  Analyzing the assembly output using tools like `objdump` (g++) or `llvm-objdump` (clang++) can show whether the copy constructor was actually invoked.

**Memory Layout Implications:** Without RVO/NRVO, the function's stack frame allocates space for the returned object, requiring a copy operation which may involve heap allocation for large objects. With RVO/NRVO, the returned object is constructed directly in the caller's memory space, saving both time and memory.

**Assembly-level behavior:** With RVO/NRVO, the assembly code often lacks instructions for copying the object's data. Instead, only the construction of the object in the destination location is seen. The absence of `memcpy` or equivalent instructions is a strong indicator of a successful optimization.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
class MyClass {
public:
  std::string data;
  MyClass(const std::string& d) : data(d) { std::cout << "MyClass constructor called\n"; }
  MyClass(const MyClass& other) : data(other.data) { std::cout << "MyClass copy constructor called\n"; }
  ~MyClass() { std::cout << "MyClass destructor called\n"; }
};
MyClass createMyClass(const std::string& str) {
  MyClass obj(str);
  return obj;
}
int main() {
  MyClass myObj = createMyClass("Hello, RVO!");
  return 0;
}
